items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-30-
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-31-**Given:** A one-point space $P=\{p\}$ and the real line $\mathbb R$ with its usual topology.
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-32-
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-33-[A1] A map $f:X\to Y$ is a homotopy equivalence when it has a continuous $g:Y\to X$ with $g\circ f\simeq\operatorname{id}_X$ and $f\circ g\simeq\operatorname{id}_Y$ ([[def-homotopy-equivalence]]).
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-34-
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md:35:[L1] The real line is a nonempty convex subset of itself, hence is contractible, and its identity is homotopic to the constant map at $0$ ([[cor-convex-subsets-of-rn-are-contractible]], [[cor-contractible-iff-identity-nullhomotopic]]).
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-36-
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-37-[L2] A homeomorphism is in particular a bijection ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-38-
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-39-[L3] The set $\mathbb R$ is uncountable, whereas a singleton is finite ([[thm-r-uncountable]]).
items/fs-homotopy-equivalent-spaces-are-homeomorphic.md-40-
